Branch-local material for REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW ... WHERE ...
================================================================

This directory is NOT part of any test schedule and is not wired into either
build system.  It exists on this branch only; the submission branch deletes it.

It was deleted once, when the feature looked finished, and restored when the
feature turned out not to be.  What came back is the subset that still has a
job now that a partial refresh is one algorithm under one spelling.  What did
not come back is listed at the bottom, with the reason, so nobody goes looking.

  ISSUES.md     Review items, split into those raised on the -hackers thread
                (A1-A9) and those found separately, each mapped to the test
                that covers it, plus a third section recording two rejected
                designs -- per-key advisory locks, built and abandoned on the
                thread, and MERGE, which was not.  Start here.

  PLAN.md       Seven phases.  Phases 1-3 are the substance; 4-7 carry it to a
                posted patch.  Written before the bare form was dropped, so
                read its routing and lock-level passages as history.

  SAFETY.md     Which predicates are safe, established by differential testing
                rather than by argument.  Five independent failure modes, what
                the planner's own push-down analysis already decides, and what
                a static check would cost.  Unaffected by the removal.

  RESULTS.md    Every measured number with the protocol that produced it, and
                a retractions section.  Its rule -- a figure may not be quoted
                without its protocol -- is the one thing here worth copying.

  USE-CASES.md  53 use cases across 17 domains against a measured cost model.
                Its "form S" column is stale: it was written when the bare
                spelling meant diff/merge, which is gone.

All timings come from a -O2 build with assertions off unless they say
otherwise.  Do not add one without recording which build produced it.


Running any of it
-----------------

Set PSQL_BIN.  A psql from $PATH is not necessarily this tree's, and a system
one several majors older connects happily and then differs in ways that read
as server behaviour.  Both harnesses now check the client against the server
and refuse a mismatch, which cost a run to learn:

    PSQL_BIN=/home/user/pgsql-opt/bin/psql ./safety/run.sh 5432 postgres
    PSQL_BIN=/home/user/pgsql-opt/bin/psql ./fuzz.sh 5432 postgres


The instruments
---------------

Every detector here has been shown to fail against a bug it is meant to catch.
That is the entry requirement, not a nicety.  Several of them did not, at
first, and looked exactly like working tests.

  safety/run.sh       The single-session differential oracle.  For each (view
                      shape, predicate, mutation): mutate the base, partial
                      refresh, snapshot, full refresh, symmetric difference.
                      23 exhaustive shapes over a 12-row base table, 1636
                      mutations, plus 42 single-shot triples carrying the
                      planner-signal measurement.  Answers "is this predicate
                      safe".

  fuzz.sh             The concurrent fuzzer, and the only thing here that can
                      see a bug needing two sessions.  Four modes: p2 and p3
                      for lock ordering over existing and inserted rows,
                      serial for whether overlapping refreshes actually
                      serialize, nest for a refresh reached from inside one.

  mutations.py        Re-introduces known bugs one at a time, defined as edits
                      to `git show HEAD:` rather than to a copy kept
                      somewhere.  33 mutations; `--check` reports every stale
                      anchor.  Note what --check does NOT do: it verifies the
                      anchor matches, not that the result compiles.  P1 sat in
                      the corpus naming two functions that do not exist, and
                      then a second time, still naming the walker by the name
                      it had before the privilege gate renamed it -- caught
                      only by building it.  P1-P7 take that gate apart one
                      check at a time (RESULTS.md R52).

  rebuild.sh          Rebuilds and then proves the rebuild happened, by asking
                      the running server what it was compiled with.  autodepend
                      is empty in this tree, so a configure change relinks
                      stale objects and exits 0.

  calibrate.sh        Measures which known bugs the oracle detects, against
  calibrate-fuzz.sh   calibrate.baseline rather than a hand-written list of
  calibrate-all.sh    shapes.  The list that existed before this did omit the
                      one shape whose behaviour was unexplained.

  leakcheck.sh        Counts plansources and memory contexts across four
                      refresh patterns.  Calibrated against L3/L4/L5.


State against the current code
------------------------------

  mutations.py --check           33/33, and P1-P7 calibrated by building
                                 and running each: 7 of 7 CAUGHT
  safety/run.sh                  23 shapes, vector identical to
                                 calibrate.baseline in every cell but one
  fuzz.sh                        PASS on p2, p3, serial and nest

The one moved cell is `two_ukeys`, which goes from 0 errors to 48: a matview
with a second unique index refuses a WHERE clause now rather than routing to
diff/merge.  The baseline is re-recorded with that in it.  Every other shape's
divergence count is unchanged, which is the evidence that dropping the bare
form and the diff/merge predicate path changed predicate safety nowhere.


What did not come back, and why
-------------------------------

Recoverable from 26ef606, which is where this directory was pinned before the
deletion.  Nothing below is lost; it is just not useful against current code.

  bench/              The benchmark suite.  Phase 6 work, not correctness
                      work, and half its arms are the bare form.
  profile.py          In-backend INSTR_TIME instrumentation.  Same reason.
  safety/rundiff.sh   Differential mode: two implementations, diffed against
                      each other.  There is one implementation now.
  safety/diff_driver.sql   Its driver.
  run.sh              The original A5 lock-ordering reproducer, superseded by
                      fuzz.sh's p2 mode.  Kept for a while because the thread
                      refers to it; the thread can refer to p2.
  CACHE.md            The subplan behind PLAN.md 3.7, which is done.
  SPECIALIZE.md       The Phase 4 specialisation design.  Two of the four
                      specialisations it chose are gone with the bare form.
  RESTORE.md          Restore points, whose build recipes predate all of this.

  mutations N1-N4     The prune elision.  Removed with the lock that made it
                      sound; there is no guard left to mutate.  The cases they
                      calibrated are still in matview_where Test 17.
  mutation Q2         diff/merge's identifier quoting on the predicate path.
                      The call site survives for the full concurrent refresh,
                      but nothing in this feature's suite reaches it, so the
                      mutation would sit in the matrix as permanently
                      UNCAUGHT -- which reads as a gap in the tests rather
                      than as what it is.


Which instrument fits which failure
-----------------------------------

This is the thing the directory keeps having to relearn, and it comes down to
one distinction:

    a rate      -> fuzz.sh.  Run it enough times and it happens.
    a window    -> an injection point.  It is microseconds wide; run it a
                   million times and it still will not happen by chance.

That was got wrong three times.  The deterministic gates for the windows live
in src/test/modules/injection_points/specs/, which ships.
